<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.6.5: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01838</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.6.5: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>872ae20a-77fe-48ce-8491-1ce4994a9cd5</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<section class="check-understanding"><exercise data-content-id="9e8e1ffb-b4d8-4207-835d-5b316853f320" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>The height of a diver above the water is given by \(h(t)=-5t^2+10t+3\), where \(t\) is time measured in seconds and \(h(t)\) is measured in meters. <emphasis>Select two</emphasis> statements that are true about the situation.</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! The diver begins 3 meters above the water. The function has 1 zero that makes sense in this situation.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activity 7.6.2. </para><para>The correct answers are: The diver begins 3 meters above the water. The function has 1 zero that makes sense in this situation. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="726e7253-2340-4293-993b-3e75356a6cf8" class="multiple-choice"><problem><newline/><para>The height of a baseball, in feet, is modeled by the function \(h\) given by the equation \(h(t)=3+60t-16t^2\). The graph of the function is shown.</para>
      <para> About when does the baseball reach its maximum height? Round to the nearest second. </para>
        <para> Enter your answer here:</para>
    </problem><solution><para>Correct! The baseball reaches its maximum height just before 2 seconds.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: The baseball reaches its maximum height just before 2 seconds. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="d537365d-160b-45d7-a5fa-9a5be01e2443" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> About how high is the maximum height of the baseball? Round to the nearest ten. </para>
        </problem><solution><para> Correct! The maximum height of the baseball is about 60 feet.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: The maximum height of the baseball is about 60 feet. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="320c2962-b632-48a9-b394-d020c4943d1a" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Enter your answer here:</para><para> When does the baseball hit the ground? Round to the nearest second. </para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! The baseball hits the ground less than 4 seconds after the baseball was hit or thrown.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.3.</para><para>The correct answer is: The baseball hits the ground less than 4 seconds after the baseball was hit or thrown. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="6e732bac-e06a-4304-920f-7e278feda78f" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>The following is a graph of the functions. Use the graph to answer the questions a  –  c.</para><para>Two rocks are launched straight up in the air. The height of Rock A is given by the function \(f\), where \(f(t)=4+30t-16t^2\). The height of Rock B is given by \(g\), where \(g(t)=5+20t-16t^2\). In both functions, \(t\) is time measured in seconds and height is measured in feet.</para>
      <para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/f80174aede0833222b5c9b332dec4092d4db5bbe"/></media></figure></para>
      <para> Which graph is the height of Rock A, \(f(t)=4+30t-16t^2\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! The red graph represents Rock A.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.2 and 7.6.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: The red graph represents Rock A. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="32df0bb3-df79-459f-8973-810157436d3a" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Which graph is the height of Rock B, \(g(t)=5+20t-16t^2\)?</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! The green graph represents Rock B.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.2 and 7.6.3. </para><para>The correct answer is: The green graph represents Rock B. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="dbbc2916-59c6-466b-aa61-9630b1d5039b" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Determine which rock hits the ground first.</para>
        </problem><solution><para>Correct! Rock B hits the ground first. The graph for Rock B (\(g(t)=5+20t-16t^2)\) crosses the \(x\)-axis at about 1.5, which means it hits the ground about 1.5 seconds after launch. The graph for Rock A (\(f(t)=4+30t-16t^2)\) crosses the \(x\)-axis at about 2, which means it hits the ground about 2 seconds after launch.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.2 – 7.6.4. </para><para>The correct answer is: Rock B hits the ground first. The graph for Rock B \((g(t)=5+20t-16t^2)\) crosses the \(x\)-axis at about 1.5, which means it hits the ground about 1.5 seconds after launch. The graph for Rock A \((f(t)=4+30t-16t^2)\) crosses the \(x\)axis at about 2, which means it hits the ground about 2 seconds after launch. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="d0a5702a-7551-4600-8d0a-1d3f520bf58a" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para>Object A: \(-5t^2+25t+50\)</para><para>Each expression represents an object’s distance from the ground, in meters, as a function of time, \(t\), in seconds.</para>
      <para>Object B: \(-5t^2+50t+25\)</para>
      <para> Which object was launched with the greater vertical speed?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Object B was launched with the greater vertical speed.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.2 – 7.6.4.</para><para>The correct answer is: Object B was launched with the greater vertical speed. </para></solution></exercise><exercise data-content-id="985503e9-0ee8-451d-9cec-4cd776dfdcb0" class="multiple-choice"><problem><para> Which object was launched from the greater height?</para>
      </problem><solution><para>Correct! Object A was launched from the greater height.</para><para>Try again. Revisit activities 7.6.2 – 7.6.4.</para><para>The correct answer is: Object A was launched from the greater height. </para></solution></exercise></section></section>
